<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emperor Ruizong of Tang</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">662–716</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Li Dan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emperor Ruizong of Tang (662–716), born Li Dan, was a son of Emperor Gaozong and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-zetian">Wu Zetian</a>. Placed on the throne by his mother in 684, he reigned only as a figurehead and yielded the throne to her when she founded her own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-dynasty">Zhou dynasty</a> in 690. He reigned again from 710 to 712 before abdicating in favour of his son, Emperor Xuanzong.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emperor-ruizong-of-tang.jpg" alt="Emperor Ruizong of Tang" width="320" height="405" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Because the artists have died over 100 years, so unknown. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tang_Ruizong.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Li Dan was born on 22 June 662 as the fourth son of Emperor Gaozong and Empress Wu. His name and title changed several times in his youth: he was created Prince of Yin the year he was born, and later Prince of Yu, Prince of Ji and Prince of Xiang, before returning to Prince of Yu in 678, when his name became Li Dan. He was said to be known for humility, love for his siblings and skill in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calligraphy">calligraphy</a>. After Gaozong died in 683, his brother Emperor Zhongzong took the throne, but Empress Wu kept power as regent.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 684 Empress Dowager Wu deposed Zhongzong when he showed signs of independence and replaced him with Li Dan. He was a figurehead: his name was not on the documents issued, and he was lodged in a separate palace and forbidden to move freely or meet officials. When she once offered to hand back imperial authority he declined, knowing she did not mean it. In October 690 he ceded the throne to his mother, who founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-dynasty">Zhou dynasty</a> and made him crown prince under the title Huangsi. In 698 Li Dan offered to yield that position to his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-zhongzong-of-tang">Li Xian</a>, who was recalled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>.</p><p>After the coup of 705 restored Zhongzong, Li Dan declined the honours offered to him. Zhongzong died on 3 July 710, allegedly poisoned by Empress Wei. Princess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiping-rebellion">Taiping</a> and Li Dan's son Li Longji then launched a coup that killed Empress Wei, and they persuaded Li Dan to take the throne again. His eldest son Li Chengqi declined to be crown prince, and Li Longji was chosen on account of his accomplishments.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Tension grew between Princess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiping-rebellion">Taiping</a> and Li Longji. In September 712 Ruizong abdicated in favour of Li Longji, who became Emperor Xuanzong, but kept the title of Taishang Huang and, at Princess Taiping's suggestion, superior power. In 713 Xuanzong, suspecting a coup, acted first, killed her associates and forced her to take her own life. Ruizong then yielded power entirely. He died at Baifu Hall in 716 and was enshrined in the imperial temple.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Emperor Ruizong of Tang” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the Grand Historian by the Han historian Sima Qian, gives three stories: in one he is Li Er or <b>Li Dan</b>, an archivist and contemporary of Confucius who wrote a book in two parts at the request of Yinxi, keeper of a pass, before departing to the West.</blockquote><a href="/work/tao-te-ching">Tao Te Ching · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>After his death in 683 she deposed her son Zhongzong, kept <b>Ruizong</b> as a figurehead, crushed a Tang princes' revolt and in 690 proclaimed her own Zhou dynasty, with Luoyang, renamed Shendu, as capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/wu-zhou">Wu Zhou</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.31,108.86&amp;year=697" aria-label="Chang'an on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7319 921 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Emperor Ruizong of Tang"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7769 1191h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7769 1191h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.31,108.86&amp;year=697">What was here in 697 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
